  "problem": "![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14011175/assets/748abf694eac3c4e59fd09d3bc48648fe30e46561f536f9f9a16eac7eb526558.png)\n\nConsider the function $y=f(x)$ whose graph is shown above. What are the $x$-coordinates of all the critical points?",
  "worked_solution": "The critical points of $f(x)$ are the values of $x$ where ${f}^{\u2032}(x)=0$ or ${f}^{\u2032}(x)$ doesn't exist. Graphically, those are the values where the tangent to the graph is horizontal or where the tangent does not exist, respectively.\n\nIn the given graph, we can see that\n\n- at any point between  $x=1$ and $x=5$ the tangent to the graph is horizontal, so we have ${f}^{\u2032}(x)=0,$ and\n\n- at $x=1$ and at $x=5$  there are sharp corners, hence ${f}^{\u2032}(1)$ and ${f}^{\u2032}(5)$ don't exist.\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14011175/assets/48b786a6b9c270336ce507525acf2503439b2424dba59ed9ea200830a79dd828.png)\n\n  \n\nTherefore, all numbers in the closed interval $[1,5]$ are critical points for $f(x).$",
